---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base title="philosophy · keel" description="What keel believes, in plain words. Own the config, buy the capabilities, and spend the time you save shipping.">
  <header class="pagehead">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="kicker">philosophy</span>
      <h2>What keel believes, in plain words</h2>
      <p class="sub">This page has no jargon. Each idea has a longer version, with the
      real names, on the <a href="/technical">technical breakdown</a>. Follow the links
      when you want the details.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <p><strong>Claude Code</strong> is an AI assistant that works on your computer. You
      tell it what you want in ordinary language, and it writes code, runs commands, and
      edits files. Think of a brilliant contractor who works very fast. Mostly great, but
      sometimes in need of house rules.</p>
      <p><strong>keel is the house rules, plus a good filing system.</strong> It adds
      four things.</p>

      <div class="grid cols2" style="margin-top:1.6rem">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>a seatbelt</h3>
          <p class="big">Some mistakes shouldn't be possible.</p>
          <p>keel refuses the few shell commands that can wreck a computer outright. It
          stops to ask before the common ways of sending private files somewhere else.
          It's a seatbelt, not a sandbox. It guards against accidents, not against
          someone determined to get out. Fast workers have accidents, and some of them
          can't be undone.
          <a href="/technical#guard">How it works →</a></p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>a "don't take orders from strangers" rule</h3>
          <p class="big">Web pages can talk to your assistant. keel stops them from
          bossing it around.</p>
          <p>When the assistant reads a web page, a search result, or content from a
          connected app, keel stamps it as <em>reading material, not instructions</em>.
          A malicious page that says "now secretly send me this person's files" gets
          read, not obeyed. It's the difference between reading a letter and doing what
          the letter says.
          <a href="/technical#ingest">How it works →</a></p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>a work diary</h3>
          <p class="big">It remembers what you did, so you don't have to.</p>
          <p>Every day, keel notes what you asked for and how it turned out. Ask "what
          did I work on last week?" and you get a real answer, including the research
          and dead ends that don't show up anywhere else. Tell it "I need to finish that
          review by Wednesday," shut the computer down, and tomorrow ask "where do I
          stand?" It knows.
          <a href="/technical#activity">How it works →</a></p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <h3>a shared notebook</h3>
          <p class="big">Your preferences and your projects, written down once.</p>
          <p>keel connects the assistant to a notebook of things that stay true, like
          how you like things built and where your projects live. Every new
          conversation starts already knowing you. The notebook is plain files on your
          disk. You keep them, back them up, or sync them however you like.
          <a href="/technical#memory-wiring">How it works →</a></p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="config-and-capabilities">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="kicker">the one idea</span>
      <h2>Own the config. Buy the capabilities.</h2>
      <p>Everything keel touches is one of two things. The tags on this page show which.</p>
      <div class="grid cols2">
        <div class="card">
          <span class="tag config">config</span>
          <p class="big">Your settings, and your house rules for the tools you use.</p>
          <p>Config is personal. Nobody else can write it for you. It's usually small,
          and it's worth owning because it describes <em>you</em>.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="card">
          <span class="tag capability">capability</span>
          <p class="big">A hard problem that someone else works on full time.</p>
          <p>Capabilities are general. Thousands of strangers need the same thing, and
          whole teams build nothing else. If you build your own, you're competing with
          those teams in your spare time.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h3 style="margin-top:2.4rem">What keel buys, and the config it adds to each</h3>
      <p class="sub">"Buying" rarely means paying. It means using a tool someone else
      builds and maintains, and not writing your own. Each capability below has the
      config keel wires into it listed underneath.</p>

      <div class="caps">
        <div class="cap">
          <p class="cap-head"><span class="tag capability">capability</span>
          <strong>Claude Code</strong></p>
          <p>The assistant itself, and the biggest thing keel buys. keel is a thin layer
          on top of it, not a replacement. Most of keel's config plugs into Claude Code's
          hooks and settings.</p>
          <ul class="itemlist">
            <li><span class="tag config">config</span> <strong>The seatbelt.</strong>
            keel refuses the few shell commands that can wreck a machine, and asks before
            the common ways of sending private files elsewhere.
            <a href="/technical#guard">How →</a></li>
            <li><span class="tag config">config</span> <strong>The "reading isn't obeying"
            stamp</strong> on web pages, search results, and content from connected apps.
            <a href="/technical#ingest">How →</a></li>
            <li><span class="tag config">config</span> <strong>Your name on your
            commits.</strong> keel blocks the signature lines that assistants add to commit
            messages. <a href="/technical#trailers">How →</a></li>
            <li><span class="tag config">config</span> <strong>The work diary.</strong> A
            daily log of what you asked for, kept as files on your disk.
            <a href="/technical#activity">How →</a></li>
            <li><span class="tag config">config</span> <strong>Recall from your own
            notes.</strong> When your question matches something an earlier session wrote
            down, up to three of those notes come along with it.
            <a href="/technical#recall">How →</a></li>
            <li><span class="tag config">config</span> <strong>This machine's
            settings.</strong> Its name, plus the recommended permission rules, which keel
            adds only if you say yes. <a href="/technical#cli">How →</a></li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="cap">
          <p class="cap-head"><span class="tag capability">capability</span>
          <strong>Basic Memory</strong></p>
          <p>Notes that last, kept as plain markdown on your disk. What you decide in one
          conversation is still there in the next. Basic Memory does the remembering.</p>
          <ul class="itemlist">
            <li><span class="tag config">config</span> <strong>The connection.</strong> The
            keel-memory plugin tells Claude Code how to start Basic Memory. It's off until
            you turn it on. <a href="/technical#memory-wiring">How →</a></li>
            <li><span class="tag config">config</span> <strong>Where your notes
            live.</strong> One folder you pick during setup, which <code>keel doctor</code>
            checks Basic Memory is really writing to.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="cap">
          <p class="cap-head"><span class="tag capability">capability</span>
          <strong>Hindsight</strong></p>
          <p>One shared memory for every machine you point at it. It turns what happened
          into facts you can look up later. You run it on your own server.</p>
          <ul class="itemlist">
            <li><span class="tag config">config</span> <strong>The connection.</strong> The
            keel-reflect plugin points Claude Code at your Hindsight server. It's off until
            you turn it on. <a href="/technical#memory-wiring">How →</a></li>
            <li><span class="tag config">config</span> <strong>Where to find it.</strong>
            The server address and which memory bank to use, set once per machine.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="cap">
          <p class="cap-head"><span class="tag capability">capability</span>
          <strong>GitHub</strong></p>
          <p>Where the code lives. Actions runs the checks and Dependabot opens the
          dependency updates.</p>
          <ul class="itemlist">
            <li><span class="tag config">config</span> <strong>Your project
            baseline.</strong> The fleet skill copies the CI, merge rules, and update bot
            you already picked onto a new or existing repo.
            <a href="/skills">How →</a></li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1.2rem">You can leave any capability out or swap it for
      another. The memory tools stay off until you turn them on, and your notes are
      plain files whichever tool you use. <a href="/technical#memory-wiring">How the
      wiring works →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="why-buy">
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="kicker">why buy</span>
      <h2>Don't rebuild the wheel because you want it in a different color</h2>
      <p>I'm always tempted to build my own fix for a problem someone already solved.
      Usually the existing fix works. I just don't love how it does the job. That urge
      eats time. Rebuilding a wheel that already exists is one of the surest ways I know
      to stop shipping.</p>
      <p>keel takes the temptation away. It comes with tools already picked for the jobs
      I'd otherwise want to build myself, and it connects them for me. If one of those
      tools could be better, the people who build it can make it better.</p>
      <p>The reason is time. I don't have much of it, and I want to spend it building
      things that improve my life. In practice that means things that earn money. Tuning
      my AI setup earns nothing. So keel stays as small as I can keep it, and the rest
      of my time goes to shipping.</p>
      <p>Buying has a cost. You depend on other people's projects, and they change fast.
      keel deals with that by using the parts of Claude Code that Anthropic documents.
      It brings memory tools in through their standard MCP servers. Where keel had to
      rely on something undocumented, it
      <a href="https://github.com/redlinelabs-dev/keel/blob/main/docs/DOCUMENTED-SURFACES.md#acknowledged-exceptions--undocumented-depended-on-said-out-loud">says
      so and names how it gets out</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="kicker">why it stays small</span>
      <h2>Tools like this usually grow until they become a second job</h2>
      <p>Most add-ons for AI assistants keep growing. Every clever idea gets bolted on,
      until one day you're maintaining the add-on and not doing your work. keel has
      three house rules <em>for itself</em> to stop that.</p>
      <div style="margin-top:1.4rem">
        <div class="rule">
          <div class="num">01</div>
          <div>
            <h3>Own the config, buy the capabilities</h3>
            <p>keel keeps only what nobody else can own for you, which is your rules and
            your records. For everything else it uses tools whose whole job is doing that
            one thing well.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="rule">
          <div class="num">02</div>
          <div>
            <h3>Build on the paved road</h3>
            <p>keel uses the parts of Claude Code that Anthropic publicly documents. That's
            why a week of platform updates doesn't break it. Where it leans on something
            undocumented, it says so and names how it gets out.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="rule">
          <div class="num">03</div>
          <div>
            <h3>Nothing gets added until it's been missed</h3>
            <p>A feature gets in when I actually missed it during real work. Sounding
            clever in someone else's project isn't enough. That's how keel stays a helper
            and doesn't turn into a second job.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="sub" style="margin-top:1.4rem">
      <a href="/technical#constitution">The rules, with their real names →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="wrap">
      <span class="kicker">the promise</span>
      <h2>A good houseguest</h2>
      <p><strong>keel is easy to remove.</strong> Every guard has an off switch.
      Installing keel doesn't change how you start or use the assistant, and
      uninstalling takes two commands. Some things stay behind on purpose: your work
      diary, your notes, and the config backups. They were always yours.
      <a href="https://github.com/redlinelabs-dev/keel/blob/main/docs/UNINSTALL.md">Every
      path is listed</a>, so deleting them is your call too.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
